window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["langchain-chatbot"]=[{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0001","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-001-1-big-picture","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s002","q":"How can a chatbot answer questions from your own documents without training or fine-tuning a model?","a":"<p>You pass the document content to the model at request time, inside the prompt. The model's weights never change. Two ways to do it: put all the text in the prompt (prompt stuffing), or retrieve only the relevant pieces per question (RAG). This is cheaper and faster to update than fine-tuning, because changing the data means changing a file or an index, not retraining.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0002","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-001-1-big-picture","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s002","q":"Compare prompt stuffing with RAG for a document-based chatbot. How do you choose?","a":"<p>Prompt stuffing puts the whole text in every prompt. It is simple, but it fails once the data exceeds the model's context window, and you pay tokens for all the text on every call. RAG splits the data into chunks, embeds them, stores them in a vector store, and retrieves the top-k chunks per question. It scales to large data and costs less per call, but it adds moving parts (splitter, embeddings, index, retriever) and can miss the right chunk. Choose stuffing for a small, fixed document that fits comfortably in the context. Choose RAG when data is large or changes often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0003","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-001-1-big-picture","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s002","q":"What are the main stages of a basic \"chat with your own data\" web app built on OpenAI, LangChain and Flask?","a":"<p>Extract text from the inputs (PDF, web page), save or hold it, build a prompt template that contains the text plus instructions, build a chain (<code>prompt | llm</code>), expose it through a Flask route that takes a question and returns the answer, and call that route from an HTML/JavaScript front end. Each stage can be swapped independently, for example PDF extraction for a database, or Flask for Streamlit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0004","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-002-2-environment-and-api-setup","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s003","q":"Where should an OpenAI API key be stored in a Python project, and why?","a":"<p>In an environment variable, or in a <code>.env</code> file that is loaded at startup and excluded from version control (add it to <code>.gitignore</code>). Never hard-code it. Keys in source end up in Git history and leaked keys can be abused to run up your bill. In production, use the platform's secret store instead of a plain file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0005","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-002-2-environment-and-api-setup","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s003","q":"What does <code>load_dotenv()</code> from <code>python-dotenv</code> do, and in which order should it run relative to creating the OpenAI client?","a":"<p>It reads <code>KEY=value</code> lines from a <code>.env</code> file and puts them into <code>os.environ</code>. It must run before code reads the variable, such as <code>os.getenv(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\")</code> or a client that reads the key from the environment. If it runs after, the key is <code>None</code> and the client fails with an authentication error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0006","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-002-2-environment-and-api-setup","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s003","q":"How does the <code>OpenAI()</code> client in the official <code>openai</code> Python SDK find the API key if you do not pass <code>api_key</code>?","a":"<p>It reads the <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> environment variable. LangChain's OpenAI classes (<code>ChatOpenAI</code>, <code>OpenAI</code>, <code>OpenAIEmbeddings</code>) also read that variable by default, so setting it once covers both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0007","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-002-2-environment-and-api-setup","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s003","q":"What is a cheap way to test that an OpenAI key and connection work, and why limit the output?","a":"<p>Send a tiny chat completion request, for example a one-line user message with <code>max_tokens=10</code>, inside a try/except and report success or the error. The small <code>max_tokens</code> keeps the cost near zero. A key-only check (is the variable set?) is even cheaper but does not prove the key is valid.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0008","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-002-2-environment-and-api-setup","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s003","q":"Why do you catch the exception in a connection-test helper, and what kinds of failures does it separate?","a":"<p>So the check reports a clear status instead of crashing. Failures to tell apart: a missing or invalid key (authentication error), exceeded quota or rate limit, a wrong model name, and a network problem. Printing the exception message tells you which one it is.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0009","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-003-3-1-pdf-extraction","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s004","q":"In PyMuPDF (<code>import fitz</code>), how do you read the text of every page of a PDF?","a":"<p>Open the file with <code>fitz.open(path)</code>, loop over <code>range(doc.page_count)</code>, get each page with <code>doc.load_page(i)</code>, and call <code>page.get_text(\"text\")</code>. Concatenate the results, then close the document.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import fitz\n\ndoc = fitz.open(&quot;file.pdf&quot;)\ntext = &quot;&quot;\nfor i in range(doc.page_count):\n    text += doc.load_page(i).get_text(&quot;text&quot;)\ndoc.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0010","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-003-3-1-pdf-extraction","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s004","q":"Compare PyMuPDF, PyPDF2 and LangChain's <code>PyPDFLoader</code> for text extraction. When would you pick each?","a":"<p>PyMuPDF is fast and usually handles layout well. PyPDF2 (now continued as <code>pypdf</code>) is pure Python, easy to install, and fine for simple PDFs. <code>PyPDFLoader</code> is a LangChain wrapper that returns <code>Document</code> objects with page metadata, so choose it when the text goes straight into a LangChain splitter or vector store. In current LangChain, loaders live in <code>langchain_community.document_loaders</code> (older code imports them from <code>langchain.document_loaders</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0011","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-003-3-1-pdf-extraction","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s004","q":"A PDF extraction function returns an empty string for some files. What is the likely cause and the fix?","a":"<p>The PDF is probably a scan, so each page is an image with no text layer. Text extractors return nothing. Run OCR (for example Tesseract, or a cloud OCR/document-intelligence service) to get text. Other causes are encrypted PDFs and text drawn as vector outlines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0012","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-003-3-1-pdf-extraction","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s004","q":"What is the pitfall in a PDF helper that catches every exception and returns an error message string instead of raising?","a":"<p>The caller cannot tell an error from real content. If the result is then saved to a file or put in a prompt, the chatbot is built on the error text. Better to raise, or to return a status flag or <code>None</code> along with the text, so the caller can validate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0013","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-003-3-1-pdf-extraction","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s004","q":"Why open the output text file with <code>encoding='utf-8'</code> when saving extracted text?","a":"<p>Without it Python uses the platform's default encoding (for example cp1252 on some Windows setups). Characters outside that set, such as curly quotes or accented letters, raise <code>UnicodeEncodeError</code> or get corrupted. Using UTF-8 on both write and read keeps the text intact.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0014","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-003-3-1-pdf-extraction","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s004","q":"Why call <code>doc.close()</code> after reading a PDF, and how would you make that automatic?","a":"<p>Closing releases the file handle and memory. To make it automatic, use a context manager (<code>with fitz.open(path) as doc:</code>), so the document closes even if an exception occurs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0015","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-004-3-2-website-extraction-web-scraping","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s005","q":"What are the steps to pull the readable text of a web page with <code>requests</code> and BeautifulSoup?","a":"<p>Send a GET request, check that the response succeeded, parse <code>response.content</code> with <code>BeautifulSoup(..., 'html.parser')</code>, select the elements you want (such as all <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tags with <code>soup.find_all('p')</code>), join their <code>get_text()</code>, and handle errors with try/except.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import requests\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\n\nresp = requests.get(&quot;https://example.com&quot;, timeout=10)\nresp.raise_for_status()\nsoup = BeautifulSoup(resp.content, &quot;html.parser&quot;)\ntext = &quot; &quot;.join(p.get_text() for p in soup.find_all(&quot;p&quot;))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0016","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-004-3-2-website-extraction-web-scraping","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s005","q":"What is the difference between checking <code>response.status_code == 200</code> and calling <code>response.raise_for_status()</code> in <code>requests</code>?","a":"<p>The first is a manual check for exactly 200. <code>raise_for_status()</code> raises <code>requests.HTTPError</code> for any 4xx or 5xx status, so errors flow into your except block. The manual check lets other success codes (such as 204) through as failures, while <code>raise_for_status()</code> treats statuses under 400 as fine. Both need a <code>timeout</code> on the request itself, because <code>requests</code> has no default timeout and can hang forever.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0017","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-004-3-2-website-extraction-web-scraping","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s005","q":"Why should <code>requests.get</code> be called with a <code>timeout</code>?","a":"<p><code>requests</code> waits indefinitely by default. A stalled server would hang your scraper or web request thread. Pass <code>timeout=10</code> (or a <code>(connect, read)</code> tuple) and handle <code>requests.Timeout</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0018","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-004-3-2-website-extraction-web-scraping","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s005","q":"What is the trade-off between extracting only <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tags and extracting all text from a page with <code>soup.get_text()</code>?","a":"<p><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> only gives clean prose but misses headings, lists, tables and text in <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> elements. <code>get_text()</code> on the whole page captures everything but also brings menus, footers and cookie banners, plus script and style content unless you remove it. A common middle path is to remove <code>script</code>, <code>style</code>, <code>nav</code> and <code>footer</code> elements and then take the text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0019","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-004-3-2-website-extraction-web-scraping","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s005","q":"In BeautifulSoup, why remove <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> elements with <code>decompose()</code> before calling <code>get_text()</code>?","a":"<p><code>get_text()</code> returns the text inside every tag, including JavaScript code and CSS rules. Those would pollute the text the chatbot uses. <code>decompose()</code> deletes the tag and its contents from the tree.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0020","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-004-3-2-website-extraction-web-scraping","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s005","q":"A scraper using <code>requests</code> and BeautifulSoup returns an almost empty page for a modern site. Why, and what are the options?","a":"<p>The page content is rendered by JavaScript in the browser, and <code>requests</code> only fetches the initial HTML. Options: call the site's underlying JSON API, or use a headless browser (Playwright, Selenium) to render the page first. Also check whether the site blocks bots.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0021","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-004-3-2-website-extraction-web-scraping","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s005","q":"What should you check before scraping a website for a production chatbot?","a":"<p>The site's terms of service and <code>robots.txt</code>, rate limits (be polite, cache results), and whether an official API or data export exists. Scraped pages change layout without notice, so parsing code is brittle and needs monitoring.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0022","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-004-3-2-website-extraction-web-scraping","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s005","q":"What does LangChain's <code>WebBaseLoader</code> do compared with hand-written requests plus BeautifulSoup code?","a":"<p>It fetches one or more URLs and returns LangChain <code>Document</code> objects (<code>page_content</code> plus metadata such as the URL). It saves you the fetch and parse code and fits directly into splitters and vector stores. Hand-written code gives finer control over which elements are kept.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0023","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-005-3-3-choosing-a-data-source","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s006","q":"When would you base a chatbot on a PDF rather than a website, and when the reverse?","a":"<p>Use a PDF for official, stable documents with a consistent layout. It needs no network after extraction and is easy to version. Use a website when the information changes often and must be current. If you need both, combine them, and decide which wins when they disagree (for example, prefer the newer one).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0024","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-005-3-3-choosing-a-data-source","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s006","q":"What are the risks of combining a PDF and a website as one knowledge base?","a":"<p>The two may contradict each other, for example an old brochure and a current web page. The model has no way to know which is newer unless you label each chunk with its origin and date, or prefer one. Also the combined text uses more tokens, so you may need RAG instead of putting everything in one prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0025","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-006-3-4-combined-collector-and-validation","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s007","q":"How would you design a data collector that tries a PDF first and falls back to a website?","a":"<p>Give each loader a return value that signals success (a boolean or <code>None</code>), keep the text in separate variables, and call the website loader only if the PDF loader reports failure. Check the file exists before opening it, wrap each step in try/except, log the reason for the fallback, and save each result to disk so later runs can skip the network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0026","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-006-3-4-combined-collector-and-validation","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s007","q":"In a collector class that stores text with <code>self.text += ...</code> inside a method, what bug appears if the method is called twice on the same object?","a":"<p>The text from the second call is appended to the first, so the content is duplicated. Reset the attribute at the start of the method (assign instead of append) or build a local string and assign it once at the end.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0027","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-006-3-4-combined-collector-and-validation","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s007","q":"Why check <code>os.path.exists(path)</code> (or <code>pathlib.Path(path).is_file()</code>) before opening a PDF, if you already have try/except?","a":"<p>It lets you report \"file not found\" as a distinct, clear case and choose the fallback, instead of treating every error the same. try/except still matters, because the file can vanish or be corrupt between the check and the open.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0028","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-006-3-4-combined-collector-and-validation","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s007","q":"When a collector reads from a PDF and a website, what is the downside of returning only the PDF text when it exists?","a":"<p>The website content is ignored even when it holds newer or extra information. A fallback strategy gives resilience, not completeness. If both matter, merge them, label where each part came from, and handle size with chunking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0029","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-007-3-4-combined-collector-and-validation","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s007","q":"What checks should you run on extracted text before using it in a chatbot prompt?","a":"<p>That it is not empty or only whitespace, that it is long enough to be meaningful, that it is not an error message from the extractor, and that the encoding is clean. Then normalize whitespace. Failing early gives a clear message instead of a chatbot that answers from nothing or invents answers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0030","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-007-3-4-combined-collector-and-validation","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s007","q":"What does <code>' '.join(text.split())</code> do, and what is the downside for some documents?","a":"<p><code>split()</code> with no argument splits on any run of whitespace, and <code>join</code> rejoins with single spaces, so it removes extra spaces, tabs and line breaks. The downside is that it also removes paragraph breaks, lists and table layout, which can hurt later chunking that relies on newlines, and which models use as structure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0031","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-007-3-4-combined-collector-and-validation","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s007","q":"What is a design smell in a validator that returns <code>(False, \"error message\")</code> or <code>(True, cleaned_text)</code>, where the second element has a different meaning in each case?","a":"<p>One slot holds two types of value (an error or the data), so callers must branch before using it and can mistake one for the other. Cleaner options: raise a <code>ValueError</code> on failure, or return a small result object or dataclass with separate <code>ok</code>, <code>text</code> and <code>error</code> fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0032","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-008-4-calling-openai-directly-vs-through-lan","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s008","q":"In the OpenAI Chat Completions API, what are the roles <code>system</code>, <code>user</code> and <code>assistant</code> used for?","a":"<p><code>system</code> sets behavior and rules for the model (task, persona, constraints). <code>user</code> carries the end user's input. <code>assistant</code> carries earlier model replies, which you send back to give the model conversation history. The API is stateless, so you resend the history on each call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0033","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-008-4-calling-openai-directly-vs-through-lan","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s008","q":"With the OpenAI Python SDK v1+, how do you read the reply text from <code>client.chat.completions.create(...)</code>?","a":"<p><code>response.choices[0].message.content</code>. <code>choices</code> is a list because you can request several completions with <code>n</code>. In SDK v1 and later you create a client with <code>OpenAI()</code> and call <code>client.chat.completions.create</code>. The older <code>openai.ChatCompletion.create</code> was removed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0034","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-008-4-calling-openai-directly-vs-through-lan","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s008","q":"What does the <code>temperature</code> parameter control, and what values would you use for an extraction task versus a creative task?","a":"<p>It controls randomness in token sampling. Low values (0 to 0.3) give more deterministic, repeatable output, suited to extraction and factual question answering. Higher values (0.7 to 1.0) give more varied output, suited to brainstorming. Even at 0 the output is not guaranteed to be identical across runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0035","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-008-4-calling-openai-directly-vs-through-lan","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s008","q":"What does <code>max_tokens</code> do in a chat completion request, and what happens if it is too small?","a":"<p>It caps the number of tokens the model may produce in the reply. If it is too small, the answer is cut off mid-sentence and the finish reason is <code>length</code>. It limits output only; the prompt size is limited by the context window. (Newer OpenAI models may expect <code>max_completion_tokens</code> instead; check the API docs for your model.)</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0036","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-008-4-calling-openai-directly-vs-through-lan","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s008","q":"Why use LangChain's <code>ChatOpenAI</code> rather than calling the OpenAI SDK directly?","a":"<p>LangChain gives one common interface (<code>invoke</code>, <code>stream</code>, <code>batch</code>) across providers, so you can swap models with little code change. It also gives prompt templates, output parsers, retrievers and the <code>|</code> composition syntax for building chains. The cost is an extra dependency, more abstraction to debug, and API changes between LangChain versions. For one simple call, the plain SDK is less code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0037","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-008-4-calling-openai-directly-vs-through-lan","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s008","q":"What does <code>llm.invoke(messages)</code> return for a LangChain <code>ChatOpenAI</code> model, and how do you get the text?","a":"<p>It returns an <code>AIMessage</code>. The text is in its <code>.content</code> attribute. A completion-style LLM such as LangChain's <code>OpenAI</code> class returns a plain string instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0038","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-008-4-calling-openai-directly-vs-through-lan","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s008","q":"What is the difference between LangChain's <code>ChatOpenAI</code> and <code>OpenAI</code> classes?","a":"<p><code>ChatOpenAI</code> uses the chat completions endpoint with a list of messages, and is the choice for chat models such as the <code>gpt-4</code> family. <code>OpenAI</code> (from <code>langchain_openai</code>) wraps the legacy text completions endpoint, takes a string prompt, and returns a string. It works only with completion-style models such as <code>gpt-3.5-turbo-instruct</code>. For new work, prefer <code>ChatOpenAI</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0039","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-008-4-calling-openai-directly-vs-through-lan","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s008","q":"In LangChain, where do <code>SystemMessage</code> and <code>HumanMessage</code> come from in current versions?","a":"<p>From <code>langchain_core.messages</code> (also re-exported by <code>langchain.schema</code> in older versions). Provider classes such as <code>ChatOpenAI</code> live in the separate <code>langchain_openai</code> package. This split into <code>langchain-core</code>, <code>langchain-community</code> and partner packages began in LangChain 0.1/0.2 and is why older tutorials' imports fail on new installs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0040","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-009-5-the-course-chatbot-persona-prompt-lang","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s009","q":"What is a persona prompt, and why add a scope limit with a fixed refusal sentence?","a":"<p>A persona prompt gives the model a role and voice (\"You are the support agent for X\"). The scope limit tells it to answer only about the supplied data and reply with a fixed sentence otherwise. This keeps answers consistent, reduces off-topic answers and hallucination, and makes refusals easy to test. It is a soft control only: the model can still be talked out of it, so add checks outside the prompt if the risk matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0041","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-009-5-the-course-chatbot-persona-prompt-lang","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s009","q":"In LangChain, what does <code>PromptTemplate(input_variables=[\"question\"], template=...)</code> do, and what happens if the template has a <code>{question}</code> placeholder that is not listed?","a":"<p>It builds a prompt with named placeholders that are filled at call time. Placeholders in the template must match the supplied variables. A mismatch raises an error at construction or at invoke (a missing variable raises a <code>KeyError</code>/validation error). In recent versions, <code>PromptTemplate.from_template(...)</code> infers the variables for you.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0042","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-009-5-the-course-chatbot-persona-prompt-lang","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s009","q":"What does the <code>|</code> operator do in <code>prompt | llm</code> in LangChain, and what do you call the result?","a":"<p>It composes runnables with LangChain Expression Language (LCEL). The output of the prompt (a formatted prompt) becomes the input of the model. The result is a runnable chain that supports <code>.invoke()</code>, <code>.stream()</code> and <code>.batch()</code>. LCEL is available from LangChain 0.1; it replaces the older <code>LLMChain</code> class, which is deprecated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0043","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-009-5-the-course-chatbot-persona-prompt-lang","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s009","q":"You build <code>chain = prompt | llm</code>. How do you run it, and what do you pass?","a":"<p>Call <code>chain.invoke({\"question\": \"...\"})</code> with a dict whose keys match the prompt's input variables.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\n\nprompt = PromptTemplate.from_template(&quot;Answer briefly: {question}&quot;)\n# chain = prompt | llm\n# answer = chain.invoke({&quot;question&quot;: &quot;What is RAG?&quot;})\nprint(prompt.format(question=&quot;What is RAG?&quot;))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0044","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-009-5-the-course-chatbot-persona-prompt-lang","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s009","q":"Why set <code>temperature=0</code> for a chatbot that must answer from supplied data?","a":"<p>It makes the model choose the most likely tokens, which gives stable, factual answers and less invention. Trade-off: replies sound less varied, and output is still not strictly deterministic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0045","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-009-5-the-course-chatbot-persona-prompt-lang","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s009","q":"What goes wrong when you concatenate raw document text into a string that is then used as a <code>PromptTemplate</code> template?","a":"<p>Curly braces in the document (JSON, code, <code>{}</code> in text) are read as placeholders. The template then fails with a missing-variable error or silently mis-formats. Pass the document as a real input variable (<code>{context}</code>) instead of splicing it into the template, or escape braces by doubling them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0046","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-009-5-the-course-chatbot-persona-prompt-lang","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s009","q":"What risk does a chatbot have when user input and trusted instructions share one prompt string?","a":"<p>Prompt injection: a user can write \"ignore the earlier rules\" and the model may follow it. Mitigations: put rules in a system message, keep the user text in its own message, validate or filter inputs and outputs, limit what the model can access, and test with adversarial prompts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0047","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-010-6-1-changes-from-the-cli-version","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s010","q":"What must change when you move a command-line chat function into a web app?","a":"<p>The function should return its answer instead of printing it, so the route can send it back. The input loop goes away because each HTTP request carries one question. State such as history must be stored explicitly, since requests are independent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0048","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-010-6-1-changes-from-the-cli-version","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s010","q":"In a Flask app, how do you create a route that accepts a JSON question and returns a JSON answer?","a":"<p>Use <code>@app.route(\"/chatbot\", methods=[\"POST\"])</code>, read the body with <code>request.get_json()</code>, and return <code>jsonify({...})</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask import Flask, request, jsonify\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(&quot;/chatbot&quot;, methods=[&quot;POST&quot;])\ndef chatbot():\n    data = request.get_json()\n    question = data[&quot;question&quot;]\n    return jsonify({&quot;response&quot;: f&quot;You asked: {question}&quot;})</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0049","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-010-6-1-changes-from-the-cli-version","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s010","q":"In a Flask chat route, what happens when the client sends a body without the expected key, and how would you handle it?","a":"<p><code>data[\"question\"]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code>, which Flask turns into a 500 error. If the body is not JSON, <code>request.get_json()</code> raises a 415 or 400 error. Validate the input: use <code>request.get_json(silent=True) or {}</code>, check the field, and return a 400 response with a message.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0050","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-010-6-1-changes-from-the-cli-version","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s010","q":"Why is <code>app.run(debug=True)</code> unsafe outside development in Flask?","a":"<p>Debug mode turns on the interactive Werkzeug debugger. Anyone who can reach the server and can get past the debugger PIN can run arbitrary Python code on the machine. It also enables the reloader and detailed tracebacks. Use <code>debug=False</code>, and run with a production WSGI server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0051","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-010-6-1-changes-from-the-cli-version","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s010","q":"Where must Flask templates be stored for <code>render_template(\"index.html\")</code> to work, and what happens otherwise?","a":"<p>In a folder named <code>templates</code> next to the application module (or the app's root path). If the file is elsewhere or the folder is misnamed (for example <code>template</code>), Flask raises <code>TemplateNotFound</code>. You can change the folder with <code>Flask(__name__, template_folder=...)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0052","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-010-6-1-changes-from-the-cli-version","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s010","q":"What does <code>Flask(__name__)</code> use the <code>__name__</code> argument for?","a":"<p>It tells Flask which module the app lives in, so it can locate the root path for templates and static files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0053","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-010-6-1-changes-from-the-cli-version","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s010","q":"Why create the LLM and chain once at import time rather than inside the route function?","a":"<p>Building clients on every request wastes time and may reload files from disk each time. A module-level chain is created once and reused. Clients are thread-safe for ordinary use, so sharing them across requests is fine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0054","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-011-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"What parts does a minimal chat page need in its HTML, and what does each do?","a":"<p>A container <code>div</code> that shows messages, a text <code>input</code> for the user's question, and a send <code>button</code>. CSS makes the container scrollable (<code>overflow-y: scroll</code> or <code>auto</code>) with a fixed height so long chats do not grow the page. A script wires the button to the code that sends the question.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0055","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-011-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"In a chat UI script, why use <code>textContent</code> instead of <code>innerHTML</code> to display a message?","a":"<p><code>textContent</code> inserts text literally. <code>innerHTML</code> parses it as HTML, so a message that contains <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> or an <code>onerror</code> attribute can run code in the page (cross-site scripting). Model output and user input are untrusted, so use <code>textContent</code> unless you sanitize.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0056","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-011-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"How do you keep a chat window scrolled to the newest message after appending a message element?","a":"<p>Set <code>container.scrollTop = container.scrollHeight</code> after <code>appendChild</code>. The container must have a fixed height with <code>overflow-y</code> set to <code>auto</code> or <code>scroll</code>, or there is nothing to scroll.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0057","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-011-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"Why <code>trim()</code> the input value and skip empty messages before sending to the server?","a":"<p>It avoids sending blank requests, which cost an API call and produce odd model replies. Check on the client for a better user experience, and also on the server, because client checks can be bypassed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0058","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-012-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"In JavaScript, how do you send a JSON question to a Flask endpoint with <code>fetch</code>?","a":"<p>Use method POST, set the <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> header, and put <code>JSON.stringify({question: message})</code> in the body. Then call <code>response.json()</code> to read the reply.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fetch(&#x27;/chatbot&#x27;, {\n  method: &#x27;POST&#x27;,\n  headers: { &#x27;Content-Type&#x27;: &#x27;application/json&#x27; },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ question: message })\n})\n  .then((response) =&gt; response.json())\n  .then((data) =&gt; console.log(data.response));</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0059","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-012-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"What happens on the Flask side if the <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> header is missing from the <code>fetch</code> request?","a":"<p><code>request.get_json()</code> refuses the body. Flask 2.3 and later raises 415 Unsupported Media Type, Flask 2.1 and 2.2 raise 400 Bad Request, and versions before 2.1 returned <code>None</code>, which later caused a confusing error. Sending the header tells Flask the body is JSON. <code>get_json(force=True)</code> ignores the header.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0060","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-012-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"Why does <code>fetch(...).then(response =&gt; response.json())</code> use two <code>.then</code> calls?","a":"<p><code>fetch</code> resolves as soon as the response headers arrive, giving a <code>Response</code> object. <code>response.json()</code> reads and parses the body, which is asynchronous and returns another promise. The second <code>.then</code> receives the parsed data. The same flow is shorter with <code>async/await</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0061","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-012-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"Does <code>fetch</code> reject its promise when the server returns a 500 status? How does that affect a chat UI's <code>.catch</code>?","a":"<p>No. <code>fetch</code> rejects only on network failure. A 4xx or 5xx response still resolves, so <code>.catch</code> will not fire. Check <code>response.ok</code> (or the status) and throw an error yourself, so the UI can show an error message.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0062","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-012-6-2-html-javascript-template-templates-i","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s011","q":"Why use a relative URL like <code>/chatbot</code> in <code>fetch</code> when the page is served by the same Flask app?","a":"<p>The browser resolves it against the page's origin, so there is no hard-coded host or port and no cross-origin (CORS) issue. It works unchanged in development and production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0063","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-013-6-3-running","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s012","q":"What is the full request path in a Flask plus LangChain chatbot, from the browser to the answer?","a":"<p>The browser's JavaScript posts JSON with <code>fetch</code> to a Flask route. The route reads the question and calls the LangChain chain. The chain formats the prompt and calls the OpenAI API. The reply goes back through the route as JSON, and the script shows it in the page. Latency is dominated by the model call, which is why a loading indicator helps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0064","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-013-6-3-running","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s012","q":"Which command starts a Flask app in development, and what URL and port does it use by default?","a":"<p><code>python main.py</code> when the file calls <code>app.run()</code>, or <code>flask run</code>, which listens on <code>http://127.0.0.1:5000</code>. On recent macOS versions port 5000 can already be used by the AirPlay Receiver, so change the port with <code>app.run(port=5001)</code> or <code>flask run --port 5001</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0065","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-013-6-3-running","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s012","q":"Why is the Flask built-in server not suitable for production, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>It is a development server: it is not built for many concurrent users, security hardening or stability. Run the app under a WSGI server such as Gunicorn (or Waitress on Windows), usually behind a reverse proxy like Nginx that handles TLS and static files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0066","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-014-7-testing-and-troubleshooting","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s013","q":"How would you test a chatbot restricted to a topic, and what makes the tests tricky?","a":"<p>Prepare a set of in-scope questions and out-of-scope questions, run each, and check the behavior: in-scope answers contain the expected facts and out-of-scope ones return the fixed refusal. Model output varies in wording, so assert on key facts or on a refusal marker rather than exact text. Use temperature 0, and for repeatable CI runs, mock the model or use an evaluation set with a judge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0067","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-014-7-testing-and-troubleshooting","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s013","q":"Your OpenAI-backed app raises an authentication error on the first call. What do you check?","a":"<p>That the environment variable is set in the same shell or process that runs the app (a variable set in another terminal is not visible), that <code>.env</code> was loaded before use, that the key has no quotes or spaces, and that the key is valid and has quota. Print only whether the key exists, never the key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0068","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-014-7-testing-and-troubleshooting","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s013","q":"Why should startup code check that required files and environment variables exist before the web server starts serving requests?","a":"<p>Failing fast with a clear message is easier to debug than a 500 error on the first user request. It also stops a deployment with missing config from looking healthy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0069","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-015-8-rag-upgrade-chunking-vector-store-retr","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s014","q":"What are the steps of a RAG pipeline for a document chatbot, in order?","a":"<p>Load documents, split them into chunks, embed each chunk, store the vectors in a vector store, then at question time embed the question, retrieve the top-k similar chunks, and pass them with the question to the LLM to answer. Indexing steps run once (or on updates); retrieval and answering run per question.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0070","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-015-8-rag-upgrade-chunking-vector-store-retr","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s014","q":"Why split documents into chunks before embedding?","a":"<p>A single embedding of a long document blurs its many topics, and retrieval would return the whole document, wasting context. Small chunks give precise matches and fit within the context window. Embedding models also have input limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0071","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-015-8-rag-upgrade-chunking-vector-store-retr","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s014","q":"What do <code>chunk_size</code> and <code>chunk_overlap</code> control in <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code>, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p><code>chunk_size</code> is the maximum chunk length (in characters by default) and <code>chunk_overlap</code> is how much consecutive chunks share. Overlap keeps a sentence or idea that sits on a boundary intact in at least one chunk. Small chunks give precise retrieval but lose context. Large chunks keep context but are less precise and use more tokens. More overlap improves continuity but increases index size and duplicate hits. Tune with real questions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0072","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-015-8-rag-upgrade-chunking-vector-store-retr","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s014","q":"What does <code>as_retriever(search_kwargs={\"k\": 3})</code> do, and what changes if you raise <code>k</code>?","a":"<p>It turns a vector store into a retriever that returns the 3 most similar chunks per query. A higher <code>k</code> raises the chance that the answer is in the context (better recall), but adds noise, token cost and latency, and can push relevant content into the middle of a long prompt where models attend less.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0073","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-015-8-rag-upgrade-chunking-vector-store-retr","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s014","q":"What does <code>chain_type=\"stuff\"</code> mean in a LangChain QA chain, and what are the alternatives?","a":"<p>\"stuff\" puts all retrieved chunks into a single prompt. It is the simplest and cheapest in calls, but is limited by the context window. Alternatives are <code>map_reduce</code> (answer per chunk, then combine), <code>refine</code> (improve the answer chunk by chunk), and <code>map_rerank</code> (score each answer). They handle more text but make more LLM calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0074","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-015-8-rag-upgrade-chunking-vector-store-retr","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s014","q":"In RAG, why must the same embedding model be used for indexing and for queries?","a":"<p>Vectors from different models live in different spaces (and may have different dimensions), so similarity scores between them are meaningless. If you change the embedding model, re-embed and rebuild the index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0075","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-015-8-rag-upgrade-chunking-vector-store-retr","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s014","q":"How does RAG reduce hallucination, and what does it not fix?","a":"<p>The model gets relevant text and is told to answer from it, so answers are grounded and can cite chunks. It does not guarantee correctness: retrieval can return the wrong chunks, the model can still ignore the context, and the answer is only as good as the data. Evaluate retrieval and answers separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0076","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-015-8-rag-upgrade-chunking-vector-store-retr","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s014","q":"Which LangChain import paths in older RAG tutorials changed in newer releases?","a":"<p>In LangChain 0.1 and later, vector stores, embeddings and loaders moved from <code>langchain.vectorstores</code>, <code>langchain.embeddings</code> and <code>langchain.document_loaders</code> to <code>langchain_community.*</code>; OpenAI embeddings and models moved to <code>langchain_openai</code>; text splitters to <code>langchain_text_splitters</code>. Check the docs for your installed version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0077","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-016-8-1-document-processing-pipeline","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s015","q":"Why is <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> the usual default splitter?","a":"<p>It tries a list of separators in order (paragraph breaks, then lines, then spaces, then characters). It splits at the largest natural boundary that keeps chunks under <code>chunk_size</code>, so chunks tend to hold whole paragraphs or sentences rather than being cut mid-word.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0078","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-016-8-1-document-processing-pipeline","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s015","q":"What is the difference between <code>split_text</code> and <code>split_documents</code> on a LangChain text splitter, and when do you use each?","a":"<p><code>split_text</code> takes a string and returns a list of strings. <code>split_documents</code> takes <code>Document</code> objects and returns <code>Document</code> chunks that keep and copy the metadata (such as page number or URL). Use <code>split_documents</code> when you need metadata for citations or filtering, and the matching <code>FAISS.from_documents</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0079","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-016-8-1-document-processing-pipeline","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s015","q":"What is the difference between <code>FAISS.from_texts</code> and <code>FAISS.from_documents</code>?","a":"<p><code>from_texts(texts, embedding)</code> takes plain strings (and optional metadata lists). <code>from_documents(docs, embedding)</code> takes <code>Document</code> objects and keeps their metadata. Both embed the content and build the index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0080","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-016-8-1-document-processing-pipeline","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s015","q":"What is FAISS, and what are the limits of using it as a vector store in a web app?","a":"<p>FAISS is a library for fast similarity search over vectors. In LangChain it runs in the app's own memory. The index is lost on restart unless saved with <code>save_local</code> and reloaded with <code>load_local</code> (which needs an explicit opt-in for deserialization, so only load files you trust). It has no built-in multi-user access, metadata filtering is limited, and updates need care. For shared or large production data, use a managed vector database such as Pinecone, pgvector, Chroma or OpenSearch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0081","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-016-8-1-document-processing-pipeline","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s015","q":"Why should the vector store be built once at startup (or offline) and not on every chat request?","a":"<p>Embedding all chunks calls the embeddings API for each chunk, which is slow and costs money. Build the index once, persist it, and only embed the question per request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0082","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-017-8-2-rag-chatbot-retrievalqa-with-a-custo","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s016","q":"In LangChain's <code>RetrievalQA.from_chain_type</code>, what is <code>chain_type_kwargs={\"prompt\": prompt}</code> for?","a":"<p>Legacy API (LangChain 0.1 to 0.3; newer code uses <code>create_retrieval_chain</code> with <code>create_stuff_documents_chain</code>). It replaces the default prompt of the \"stuff\" chain with your own. The custom prompt must use the variable names the chain supplies: <code>{context}</code> for the retrieved text and <code>{question}</code> for the user's question. Use it to add rules such as \"If the context does not contain the answer, say you don't know\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0083","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-017-8-2-rag-chatbot-retrievalqa-with-a-custo","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s016","q":"Why do RAG prompts instruct the model to say it does not know when the context lacks the answer?","a":"<p>Without that instruction the model falls back on its general knowledge or invents an answer, which defeats grounding. The instruction gives a safe failure mode. It reduces, but does not remove, the risk, so test with questions that have no answer in the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0084","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-017-8-2-rag-chatbot-retrievalqa-with-a-custo","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s016","q":"With <code>RetrievalQA</code>, what key do you pass to <code>invoke</code>, and which keys are in the result?","a":"<p>Legacy API (LangChain 0.1 to 0.3; newer retrieval chains built with <code>create_retrieval_chain</code> take <code>{\"input\": question}</code> and return <code>answer</code>). For <code>RetrievalQA</code>, pass <code>{\"query\": question}</code>. The result contains <code>result</code> (the answer) and, with <code>return_source_documents=True</code>, <code>source_documents</code> (the retrieved chunks).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># qa = RetrievalQA.from_chain_type(llm=llm, retriever=retriever,\n#                                  return_source_documents=True)\n# out = qa.invoke({&quot;query&quot;: &quot;What is covered?&quot;})\n# answer = out[&quot;result&quot;]\n# chunks = out[&quot;source_documents&quot;]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0085","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-017-8-2-rag-chatbot-retrievalqa-with-a-custo","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s016","q":"Why return the retrieved documents along with the answer in a RAG chatbot?","a":"<p>They let users and developers verify the answer, show citations, and debug retrieval. If an answer is wrong, you can see whether retrieval failed (wrong chunks) or the model misused correct chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0086","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-017-8-2-rag-chatbot-retrievalqa-with-a-custo","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s016","q":"Why catch exceptions in the chat method of a RAG bot and return a structured result with a success flag?","a":"<p>LLM calls fail for many reasons (rate limits, timeouts, network). A structured result lets the route return a clean error to the UI instead of a crash, while logging the real exception server-side. Do not send raw exception text to end users, since it can leak internal details.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0087","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-017-8-2-rag-chatbot-retrievalqa-with-a-custo","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s016","q":"What is the status of <code>RetrievalQA</code> in current LangChain, and what replaces it?","a":"<p>In LangChain 0.2 and later it is a legacy chain and the docs recommend building retrieval chains with <code>create_retrieval_chain</code> and <code>create_stuff_documents_chain</code>, or with LCEL (<code>retriever | prompt | llm | parser</code>) or LangGraph. Older tutorials still show <code>RetrievalQA</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0088","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-018-8-3-conversation-memory-conversationalre","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s017","q":"A user asks \"What is the refund policy?\" and then \"How long does it take?\". Why does plain retrieval fail on the second question, and how does <code>ConversationalRetrievalChain</code> fix it?","a":"<p>Legacy API (deprecated since LangChain 0.1.17; newer code uses <code>create_history_aware_retriever</code> with <code>create_retrieval_chain</code>, or LangGraph). The second question has no standalone meaning, so the retriever search for it finds irrelevant chunks. <code>ConversationalRetrievalChain</code> first uses the chat history and the new question to have the LLM rewrite a standalone question (\"How long does a refund take?\"), then retrieves with that and answers. It costs one extra LLM call per turn.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0089","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-018-8-3-conversation-memory-conversationalre","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s017","q":"What do <code>memory_key=\"chat_history\"</code> and <code>return_messages=True</code> mean in <code>ConversationBufferMemory</code>?","a":"<p>Legacy API (LangChain 0.1 to 0.3; <code>ConversationBufferMemory</code> is deprecated in 0.3 in favour of <code>RunnableWithMessageHistory</code> or LangGraph persistence). <code>memory_key</code> is the name of the variable under which the history is passed into the chain's prompt, so it must match the name the chain expects (<code>chat_history</code> for <code>ConversationalRetrievalChain</code>). <code>return_messages=True</code> returns the history as message objects (human and AI messages) rather than one formatted string, which is what chat models and this chain expect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0090","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-018-8-3-conversation-memory-conversationalre","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s017","q":"Why can <code>ConversationalRetrievalChain</code> with memory and <code>return_source_documents=True</code> raise an error about multiple output keys?","a":"<p>The memory needs to know which output to save. With both <code>answer</code> and <code>source_documents</code> in the output, it raises an error asking for one key. Set <code>output_key=\"answer\"</code> on the memory (<code>ConversationBufferMemory(memory_key=\"chat_history\", return_messages=True, output_key=\"answer\")</code>). Applies to the legacy memory classes in LangChain 0.1 to 0.3.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0091","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-018-8-3-conversation-memory-conversationalre","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s017","q":"What is the downside of <code>ConversationBufferMemory</code>, and what alternatives exist?","a":"<p>It stores every message, so the prompt grows each turn, which raises cost and eventually exceeds the context window. Alternatives: <code>ConversationBufferWindowMemory</code> (keep the last N turns), <code>ConversationSummaryMemory</code> (summarize old turns), and <code>VectorStoreRetrieverMemory</code> (retrieve relevant past turns). All of these classes are legacy in LangChain 0.3.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0092","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-018-8-3-conversation-memory-conversationalre","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s017","q":"A web app creates one chatbot object with its own memory at startup. What goes wrong with multiple users?","a":"<p>All users share the same history, so one person's questions and answers leak into another person's conversation (a privacy bug). Keep memory per session: store a history object per session ID, or give each client its own memory, in a store such as Redis or a database, and expire it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0093","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-018-8-3-conversation-memory-conversationalre","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s017","q":"What is the recommended way to add chat history to a chain in newer LangChain?","a":"<p>In LangChain 0.2 and later, use <code>RunnableWithMessageHistory</code> with a function that returns a chat history object per session ID. In LangChain 0.3, long-term memory for new agents goes through LangGraph persistence. The legacy <code>ConversationBufferMemory</code> classes still work but are deprecated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0094","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-019-9-alternative-interfaces-streamlit-and-a","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s018","q":"How does Streamlit's execution model affect a chatbot, and why use <code>st.session_state</code>?","a":"<p>Streamlit reruns the whole script from top to bottom on every interaction, such as a button click. Plain variables reset on each rerun. <code>st.session_state</code> keeps values (such as a chatbot object or the chat history) across reruns for that user session. Without it, memory would be lost on every click.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0095","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-019-9-alternative-interfaces-streamlit-and-a","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s018","q":"When would you choose Streamlit instead of Flask with HTML and JavaScript for a chatbot UI?","a":"<p>Streamlit suits quick internal demos and data apps in pure Python, with no front-end code. Flask with your own HTML/JS suits custom UI, branding, embedding in an existing site, and exposing an API for other clients. Streamlit gives less control over layout and has its own deployment model. Newer Streamlit versions also provide <code>st.chat_input</code> and <code>st.chat_message</code> built for chat.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0096","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-019-9-alternative-interfaces-streamlit-and-a","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s018","q":"Why does a browser front end served from a different origin need <code>flask_cors.CORS(app)</code> to call a Flask API?","a":"<p>Browsers block cross-origin requests unless the server sends CORS headers (<code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> and related). <code>CORS(app)</code> adds them. With no arguments it allows every origin, so in production restrict it with <code>CORS(app, origins=[\"https://your-site.example\"])</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0097","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-019-9-alternative-interfaces-streamlit-and-a","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s018","q":"What HTTP status code should a chat API return when the <code>question</code> field is missing or empty, and what for an unexpected server error?","a":"<p>400 Bad Request for invalid client input, and 500 Internal Server Error for unexpected failures (or 502/504 if an upstream model call fails or times out). Correct codes let clients react, and monitoring can tell client errors from server faults.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0098","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-019-9-alternative-interfaces-streamlit-and-a","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s018","q":"What is the purpose of a <code>/health</code> endpoint in a web API?","a":"<p>It is a cheap endpoint that returns 200 when the app is up. Load balancers, container orchestrators and uptime monitors call it to decide whether to send traffic or restart the instance. A deeper readiness check can also test dependencies such as the database or vector store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0099","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-019-9-alternative-interfaces-streamlit-and-a","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s018","q":"Why is <code>app.run(host='0.0.0.0')</code> a concern, and when do you use it?","a":"<p><code>0.0.0.0</code> makes the server listen on every network interface, so other machines can reach it. That is needed inside a container or to test from another device, but with debug mode on it exposes the debugger. In development keep the default <code>127.0.0.1</code>. In production, run behind a reverse proxy with authentication and TLS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0100","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-020-10-1-better-styling-and-responsive-layou","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s019","q":"How do you make a chat message list scroll while the input stays visible at the bottom?","a":"<p>Give the message container a fixed or flexible height with <code>overflow-y: auto</code>, and put the input row outside it, for example as a sibling in a flex column layout. Only the message container scrolls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0101","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-020-10-1-better-styling-and-responsive-layou","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s019","q":"Why set <code>word-wrap: break-word</code> and a <code>max-width</code> on chat message bubbles?","a":"<p><code>max-width</code> (for example 70%) keeps bubbles from spanning the full width, which looks like a chat. <code>word-wrap: break-word</code> (or <code>overflow-wrap: anywhere</code>) breaks long unbroken strings such as URLs, so they do not overflow the bubble.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0102","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-020-10-1-better-styling-and-responsive-layou","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s019","q":"How do you align user messages to the right and bot messages to the left in CSS?","a":"<p>Make each message a block with a <code>max-width</code> and use <code>margin-left: auto</code> for user messages (pushes them right) and <code>margin-right: auto</code> for bot messages. Alternatively make the container a flex column and use <code>align-self: flex-end</code> or <code>flex-start</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0103","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-020-10-1-better-styling-and-responsive-layou","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s019","q":"What does <code>flex: 1</code> do on a text input inside a <code>display: flex</code> row with a button?","a":"<p>It lets the input take all remaining width, while the button keeps its natural size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0104","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-021-10-1-better-styling-and-responsive-layou","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s019","q":"What does a CSS rule like <code>@media (max-width: 768px) { ... }</code> do?","a":"<p>It applies the rules inside it only when the viewport is at most 768px wide, so you can adapt layout for tablets and phones, for example shrinking padding, widening message bubbles and stacking the input and button vertically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0105","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-021-10-1-better-styling-and-responsive-layou","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s019","q":"Why does a responsive page need <code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;</code>?","a":"<p>Without it, mobile browsers lay the page out at a wide desktop width (about 980px) and then scale it down, so media queries based on width never match and text is tiny. The tag tells the browser to use the real device width.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0106","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-021-10-1-better-styling-and-responsive-layou","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s019","q":"Why set the chat input's <code>font-size</code> to at least 16px on mobile?","a":"<p>Safari on iOS zooms the page when a focused input has a font size under 16px, which disrupts the layout. A 16px font prevents the automatic zoom.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0107","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-021-10-1-better-styling-and-responsive-layou","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s019","q":"What is the mobile-first approach to responsive CSS, compared with writing desktop styles and overriding them?","a":"<p>Mobile-first writes base styles for small screens and adds <code>@media (min-width: ...)</code> rules for larger ones. It keeps the default CSS simple and lightweight for phones. The desktop-first approach shown with <code>max-width</code> queries works but needs more overrides.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0108","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-022-10-2-typing-indicator-error-message-ente","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s020","q":"Why is it important to hide the typing indicator in both the success and the error paths of a request?","a":"<p>If the indicator is removed only on success, a failed request leaves \"typing...\" on screen forever. Hide it in the <code>.then</code> and the <code>.catch</code>, or use <code>.finally()</code> (or <code>try/finally</code> with <code>await</code>) so it always runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0109","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-022-10-2-typing-indicator-error-message-ente","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s020","q":"How should the chat UI behave when the server call fails, and what should the user see?","a":"<p>Show a short, friendly message in the chat (\"Sorry, something went wrong. Please try again.\"), log the technical details to the console or a server log, keep the typed text if possible, and let the user retry. Do not show raw stack traces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0110","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-022-10-2-typing-indicator-error-message-ente","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s020","q":"How do you send the message when the user presses Enter in a text input?","a":"<p>Add a <code>keydown</code> listener to the input and call the send function when <code>e.key === 'Enter'</code>. The <code>keypress</code> event is deprecated, so prefer <code>keydown</code>. Ignore Enter while an IME composition is active (<code>e.isComposing</code>) and while a request is already in flight.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0111","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-022-10-2-typing-indicator-error-message-ente","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s020","q":"Why disable the send button or input while waiting for a reply?","a":"<p>It prevents duplicate submissions and out-of-order replies when the user clicks repeatedly during a slow model call. Re-enable in the success and error paths.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0112","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-023-10-3-memory-in-the-persona-chatbot","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s021","q":"In a conversation prompt template with <code>{chat_history}</code> and <code>{input}</code>, what must match between the template, the memory and the chain?","a":"<p>The template's variable names must equal the memory's <code>memory_key</code> (here <code>chat_history</code>) and the chain's input key (<code>input</code>). A mismatch raises a validation error or leaves the history unused.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0113","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-023-10-3-memory-in-the-persona-chatbot","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s021","q":"In an f-string used to build a LangChain prompt template, why are some placeholders written as <code>{{chat_history}}</code>?","a":"<p>In an f-string, <code>{x}</code> is filled by Python immediately. Doubling the braces yields a literal <code>{chat_history}</code> in the final string, which LangChain's <code>PromptTemplate</code> fills later. Using single braces would raise <code>NameError</code> or insert the wrong value.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>document = &quot;Some facts.&quot;\ntemplate = f&quot;&quot;&quot;{document}\nHistory: {{chat_history}}\nHuman: {{input}}\nAI:&quot;&quot;&quot;\nprint(template)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0114","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-023-10-3-memory-in-the-persona-chatbot","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s021","q":"In LangChain, how does <code>chain.predict(input=...)</code> differ from <code>chain.invoke({...})</code> on a legacy chain such as <code>ConversationChain</code>?","a":"<p><code>predict</code> is a legacy convenience method that takes keyword arguments and returns only the output text as a string. <code>invoke</code> is the standard Runnable method (LangChain 0.1 and later): it takes a dict and returns a dict of outputs, or the output itself for runnables such as <code>prompt | llm</code>. Use <code>invoke</code>, because <code>predict</code> and <code>ConversationChain</code> are deprecated (since LangChain 0.2.7).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0115","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-024-10-4-multiple-sources-with-vector-search","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s022","q":"What does <code>vectorstore.similarity_search(question, k=3)</code> return, and how would you use it without a prebuilt chain?","a":"<p>It returns the 3 <code>Document</code> objects whose embeddings are closest to the question's embedding. Join their <code>page_content</code> into a context string, put it into a prompt with the question, and call the LLM yourself. This gives full control over the prompt and flow at the cost of writing the glue code.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def build_prompt(docs, question):\n    context = &quot;\\n&quot;.join(d.page_content for d in docs)\n    return f&quot;Context: {context}\\nQuestion: {question}\\nAnswer:&quot;\n\n# docs = vectorstore.similarity_search(question, k=3)\n# answer = llm.invoke(build_prompt(docs, question))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0116","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-024-10-4-multiple-sources-with-vector-search","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s022","q":"Why would you build retrieval by hand instead of using <code>RetrievalQA</code>, and what do you give up?","a":"<p>You control exactly how retrieval, prompt and post-processing work, which helps with debugging and custom logic (filters, reranking, citations), and it avoids deprecated chain classes. You give up ready-made features such as source-document return and chain-level callbacks, and you write more code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0117","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-024-10-4-multiple-sources-with-vector-search","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s022","q":"How do you search chunks that come from two different inputs, such as a PDF and a web page, and still tell where each came from?","a":"<p>Load both with LangChain loaders into <code>Document</code> objects, split with <code>split_documents</code> so metadata is preserved, and store them in one vector store. Each chunk keeps its <code>metadata</code> (page, URL). You can show it for citations or filter on it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0118","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-024-10-4-multiple-sources-with-vector-search","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s022","q":"In LangChain, what is wrong with calling an LLM object like a function, as in <code>llm(prompt)</code>, in current versions?","a":"<p>Direct calls of an LLM object were deprecated in langchain-core 0.1.7 and still works with a warning in 0.2 and 0.3, and removal is planned for 1.0. Use <code>llm.invoke(prompt)</code>. Note that <code>invoke</code> on a chat model returns a message with <code>.content</code>, while on a completion LLM it returns a string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0119","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-025-10-5-sentiment-adaptive-tone","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s023","q":"How would you adapt a chatbot's tone to the user's sentiment?","a":"<p>Detect sentiment on the user message (a library such as TextBlob, a classifier, or an LLM call), then change behavior. Either wrap the answer with fixed phrases, or better, pass the sentiment into the system prompt (\"The user seems upset: be empathetic and concise\") so the model adapts wording naturally. Fixed text wrappers are easy but sound mechanical.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0120","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-025-10-5-sentiment-adaptive-tone","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s023","q":"What does TextBlob's <code>sentiment.polarity</code> return, and why are thresholds such as ±0.1 used?","a":"<p>Polarity is a float from -1.0 (negative) to +1.0 (positive). A small band around zero is treated as neutral, so mildly worded or ambiguous text is not misclassified. Choose the band by testing on real messages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0121","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-025-10-5-sentiment-adaptive-tone","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s023","q":"What are the limits of lexicon-based sentiment such as TextBlob for a chatbot?","a":"<p>It scores words without understanding context, so it misses sarcasm, negation in complex sentences, domain wording and other languages. For better accuracy, use a trained classifier or ask an LLM, at the cost of extra latency and spend.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0122","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-026-10-6-real-time-chat-websocket","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s024","q":"Compare HTTP request/response with WebSockets for a chat app. When is each appropriate?","a":"<p>HTTP is one request and one reply, started by the client. A WebSocket is a persistent, two-way connection, so the server can push messages at any time, for example streamed tokens, typing events and notifications. For a simple question and answer chatbot, plain HTTP is enough and simpler to scale. Use WebSockets when you need server push, streaming, or multi-user live chat.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0123","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-026-10-6-real-time-chat-websocket","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s024","q":"In Flask-SocketIO, what do <code>@socketio.on('message')</code> and <code>emit('response', ...)</code> do?","a":"<p><code>@socketio.on('message')</code> registers a handler that runs when a client emits an event named <code>message</code>. <code>emit('response', data)</code> sends an event named <code>response</code> back to that client, and the client listens with <code>socket.on('response', ...)</code>. Event names must match on both sides.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0124","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-026-10-6-real-time-chat-websocket","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s024","q":"Why is <code>cors_allowed_origins=\"*\"</code> risky in a Flask-SocketIO app?","a":"<p>It lets pages from any website open socket connections to your server from a visitor's browser. In production, list your own origins explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0125","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-026-10-6-real-time-chat-websocket","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s024","q":"How do you run a Flask-SocketIO app, and why is <code>app.run()</code> not the right call?","a":"<p>Use <code>socketio.run(app)</code>, which starts a server with WebSocket support. In production, run under an async-capable worker (eventlet or gevent, or a threading mode with a suitable server such as Gunicorn). The plain Flask development server does not provide production WebSocket handling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0126","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-026-10-6-real-time-chat-websocket","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s024","q":"A real-time chatbot calls a slow LLM inside a socket handler. What problem can this cause, and how can you improve it?","a":"<p>A blocking call ties up the worker, so other connections wait. Use a background task or async worker, and stream tokens as they arrive (<code>llm.stream(...)</code>, emitting each chunk) so the user sees text sooner.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0127","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-027-10-7-usage-analytics","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s025","q":"What goes wrong when a web app updates a JSON file with counts on every request, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>Concurrent requests read and write the file at the same time, so updates are lost or the file is corrupted, and every request pays for disk I/O. Use a database (SQLite for small apps, PostgreSQL for more), an atomic counter store such as Redis, or a metrics or logging system.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0128","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-027-10-7-usage-analytics","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s025","q":"What privacy concerns come with logging user questions in a chatbot?","a":"<p>Questions can contain personal or sensitive data. Decide what to store, redact PII, define retention, secure access, and tell users in a privacy notice. Prefer aggregate counts to raw text when that is enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0129","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-027-10-7-usage-analytics","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s025","q":"Which metrics are useful to track for an LLM chatbot in production?","a":"<p>Request counts, latency (total and model call), error and timeout rates, token usage and cost per request, refusal or fallback rate, retrieval hit quality for RAG, and user feedback ratings. Latency percentiles (p95, p99) say more than averages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0130","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-027-10-7-usage-analytics","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s025","q":"In Python, how would you measure the response time of a request handler, and what does a <code>NameError: name 'time' is not defined</code> mean here?","a":"<p>Record <code>time.perf_counter()</code> before and after the call and subtract. The <code>NameError</code> means <code>import time</code> is missing at the top of the module.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import time\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\n# answer = chain.invoke({&quot;question&quot;: question})\nelapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\nprint(f&quot;{elapsed:.3f}s&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0131","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-027-10-7-usage-analytics","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s025","q":"Why would an analytics dictionary keyed by raw question text become a problem over time?","a":"<p>Every distinct question adds a key, so it grows without bound, and near-duplicates (\"hi\", \"Hi!\") are counted separately. Normalize the text, cap or prune the data, or store it in a database with periodic aggregation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0132","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-028-10-8-production-configuration-config-cla","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s026","q":"What is the Flask application factory pattern, and why use it?","a":"<p>A function such as <code>create_app(config)</code> that builds and returns a <code>Flask</code> app, instead of creating a global <code>app</code> at import time. It lets you create several app instances with different configs (testing, development, production), avoids circular imports, and makes extension setup and blueprint registration explicit and testable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0133","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-028-10-8-production-configuration-config-cla","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s026","q":"How do config classes with <code>app.config.from_object(...)</code> work in Flask?","a":"<p><code>from_object</code> copies the uppercase attributes of a class (or module) into <code>app.config</code>. A base <code>Config</code> holds shared settings, and subclasses such as <code>DevelopmentConfig</code> and <code>ProductionConfig</code> override values like <code>DEBUG</code>. The factory takes the class to use, so the environment chooses the settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0134","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-028-10-8-production-configuration-config-cla","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s026","q":"What is the risk of <code>SECRET_KEY = os.environ.get('SECRET_KEY') or 'dev-secret-key'</code> in a production config?","a":"<p>If the environment variable is missing, the app silently runs with a well-known default. Anyone who knows it can forge signed session cookies. In production config, fail at startup when the variable is missing instead of falling back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0135","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-028-10-8-production-configuration-config-cla","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s026","q":"What are Flask blueprints, and why does an app factory register them?","a":"<p>A blueprint groups related routes, templates and static files into a module. The factory calls <code>app.register_blueprint(...)</code>, which attaches those routes to the app instance it just built, so routes are not tied to a global app object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0136","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-028-10-8-production-configuration-config-cla","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s026","q":"What differences should a production configuration have from a development one for a Flask chatbot?","a":"<p><code>DEBUG</code> off, secrets from the environment or a secret manager, a production WSGI server (Gunicorn) behind a reverse proxy with HTTPS, restricted CORS, structured logging, rate limiting on the chat endpoint to control OpenAI cost, and a health endpoint. Set the environment through config, not code edits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"langchain-chatbot-f0137","card":"q-langchain-chatbot-028-10-8-production-configuration-config-cla","sec":"langchain-chatbot-s026","q":"Why add rate limiting to a public chatbot endpoint?","a":"<p>Each request calls a paid API, so abuse or a bug can run up cost and exhaust your quota. A limiter (for example Flask-Limiter, or limits at the API gateway) caps requests per client, and an input length limit caps tokens per request.</p>","hand":true}];
